  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is an idler gear for?",
      "a": "An idler gear sits between the driver and the driven gear. It makes the driven gear turn the same way as the driver, and it can bridge a gap between gears that are far apart.",
      "asks": [
        "Why add a middle gear?",
        "What does the middle gear do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does an idler gear change the speed?",
      "a": "No. Every tooth the driver moves passes through the idler unchanged, so the final speed depends only on the driver and driven gears. A big or small idler gives the same result.",
      "asks": [
        "Does the size of the idler matter?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Which way does the driven gear turn?",
      "a": "With one idler in between, the driven gear turns the same way as the driver. With no idler, or with two idlers, it turns the opposite way.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the last gear turn the same way?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What turns the driver gear?",
      "a": "Something outside the gear train turns the driver: here a hand crank, but often a motor or a spring. Every other gear is pushed round by teeth.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does the motion come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can I tell which way the last gear turns?",
      "a": "Count the gears. In a single line of meshing gears, odd-numbered gears turn the same way as the first and even-numbered gears turn the opposite way.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you work out gear direction?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In a simple gear train, an idler gear does not change the overall gear ratio.",
    "In a line of meshing gears, the first, third and fifth gears all turn the same way."
  ],
